{
  "id": "relikd-interrupt-pattern-database",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Mirror and alternating-group interrupt scores",
  "question": "Do the committed LiberPrayground mirror and modulo interrupt scores reproduce, and can their structural scores identify a known period?",
  "reader_question": "Did the saved mirror and alternating-group scores uncover a message?",
  "summary": "All 7,407 committed candidate scores reproduce to displayed precision. On a planted known reading, the true period leads 22 of 24 source-rule checks, but these scores neither discover skip positions nor decode LP2.",
  "finding": "The four mirror and twenty modulo source database files contain 7,407 candidate rows across nine sections; independent source-class replay agrees within 0.000005 for every saved score. A 600-rune known-text period-5 control leads in 22/24 arms. In a post-target diagnostic, 19/24 planted scores exceed all 49 matched whole-period-search shuffles.",
  "limitations": "The committed rows do not supply an independently verified historic execution log, and we did not rerun the 2^20 skip-mask selection that produced the saved maxima. The known-text controls are given the correct F positions and test structural period detection, not complete-message decryption. Two modulo-b normal-frequency controls miss the true period. The 49 shuffles were added after LP2 score inspection and are not a selection-adjusted LP2 null. No cipher family is excluded or identified.",
  "next_steps": "Before interpreting an interrupt/key proposal as a reading, demonstrate complete known-message recovery with the skip positions inferred rather than supplied, and compare the entire selection procedure with matched controls.",
  "outcome": "inconclusive",
